<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.12.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01069</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.12.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>e7dab090-d076-40f9-8b45-c382b12b718e</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para><emphasis><emphasis>Students will complete the following questions to practice the skills they have learned in this lesson.</emphasis></emphasis></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
<item>The cost for an upcoming field trip is $15 per student. The cost of the field trip \(C\), in dollars, is a function of the number of students, \(x\).</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Select two</emphasis> possible outputs for the function defined by \(C(x)=15x\).</para>
<list>
    <item>
      20
    </item>
    <item>
      30
    </item>
    <item>
      50
    </item>
    <item>
      90
    </item>
    <item>
      100
    </item>
  </list>

  <para><emphasis>Answers:</emphasis> 30, 90</para>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
<item>A rectangle has an area of  \(24   cm^2\). Function \(f\) gives the length of the rectangle, in centimeters when the width is \(w\) cm.</item>
</list>
<para>Determine which values, in centimeters, are possible inputs of the function.</para>
<para><emphasis>Select three </emphasis> possible that apply.</para>
<list>
    <item>
      3
    </item>
    <item>
      0.5
    </item>
    <item>
      48
    </item>
    <item>–6
    </item>
    <item>
      0
    </item>
  </list>

  <para><emphasis>Answers:</emphasis> 3, 0.5, 48</para>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
<item><emphasis>Select three</emphasis> possible input-output pairs for the function \(y=x^3\).</item>
</list>
<list>
    <item><span>\((-1,-1)\)</span></item>
    <item>
      \((-2,8)\)</item>
    <item>
      \((3,9)\)</item>
    <item>
      \((\frac12,\frac18)\)
    </item>
    <item><span>
        \((4,64)\)</span></item>
    <item>
      \((1,-1)\)</item>
    </list>

  <para><emphasis>Answers: </emphasis>(-1,-1), \((\frac12,\frac18)\), (4,64)</para>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>Two functions are defined by the equations \(f(x)=5−0.2x\) and \(g(x)=0.2(x+5)\).</item>
</list>

  <para><emphasis>Select two </emphasis>possible statements that are true about the functions.</para>

<list>
    <item>
      \(f(3)&gt;0\)
    </item>
    <item>\(
      f(3)&gt;5\)
    </item>
    <item>
      \(g(-1)=0.8\)
    </item>
    <item>\(
      f(0)=g(0)\)
    </item>
  </list>

  <para><emphasis>Answers: </emphasis>\(f(3)&gt;0\), \(g(-1)=0.8\)</para>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>Is the relation graphed below a function?</item>
</list>


  <para><figure><media alt="The figure shows the graph of some points on the x y-coordinate plane. The x and y-axes run from negative 6 to 6. The points (negative 3, 4), (negative 3, negative 1), (0, negative 3), (2, 3), (4, negative 1), and (4, negative 3)."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/944cfd8f63cb30c6cde5373d42c53e3103f39d39"/></media></figure></para>
  <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> No; this is not a function because 4 maps to both –1 and –3.</para>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item>Is the relation graphed below a function?</item>
</list>

  <para><figure><media alt="The figure shows the graph of some points on the x y-coordinate plane. The x and y-axes run from negative 6 to 6. The points (negative 3, 4), (negative 3, negative 4), (negative 2, 0), (negative 1, 3), (1, 5), and (4, negative 2)."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/c26a999a0d0a7ad911c353f0086dbd924ea0ba97"/></media></figure></para>
  <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>No; this is not a function because –3 maps to both 4 and –4.</para>

<para>Use the following scenario for questions 7 - 9.</para>
<blockquote>
  <para>For a fundraiser, Chi and her class are having a talent show and charging $7 a ticket. There are 100 seats in the theater. The function \(T\) gives the total for selling \(n\) tickets and is represented by \(T(n)=7n\).</para>
</blockquote>  
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item>Enter the minimum domain value.
  </item>
</list>

  <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 0; The minimum domain value is 0.</para>
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
    <item>
      Enter the maximum domain value.
    </item>
  </list>
  <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 100; The maximum domain value is 100.</para>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="9" type="enumerated">
  <item> Is 30 a possible output in the range?</item>
</list>

  <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> No; 30 is not a possible value in the range because all values of the range must be multiples of 7.</para>
</section>
</content>
